   206	# The narrow set: changes a human must personally stop on, because no amount of
   207	# automated review buys back a mistake here.
   208	#
   209	#   1. schema migrations / raw SQL  — can destroy data; there is no undo
   210	#   2. money paths                  — a wrong number moves real funds
   211	#   3. authz / RLS / permissions    — a wrong rule shows one customer another's data
   212	#
   213	# Everything else at L3 (workers, cron, CI config, deploy wiring, concurrency,
   214	# env plumbing) is dangerous enough to earn the FULL automated loop — tests,
   215	# an independent diff gate, a red-team pass, hashed evidence — but it is
   216	# revertible, so stopping a solo founder on every one of them buys less than it
   217	# costs. This list is deliberately short: the moment it grows to cover
   218	# "anything that feels important", the stop stops meaning anything.
   219	# Which L3 REASONS are hard to take back. This is derived from the rule that
   220	# matched, not from a second regex kept in step by hand — the parallel regex
   221	# drifted from RISK_RULES three separate times (camelCase authz, then `acl`,
   222	# then alembic/db-migrate directories), each time silently waiving the human
   223	# stop for exactly the changes `--human-gate-scope irreversible` promises to
   224	# stop. A rule added above is now covered here automatically, or not at all,
   225	# and never half-covered.
   226	IRREVERSIBLE_REASONS = frozenset({
   227	    "database migration",
   228	    "raw SQL",
   229	    "authentication / authorisation / RLS",
   230	    "money path",
   231	    "schema / data model",
   232	})
